<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">René Magritte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">René Magritte was a Belgian surrealist painter whose witty images questioned the relation between pictures, words and reality. His works include &quot;The Treachery of Images&quot;, with its pipe captioned &quot;This is not a pipe&quot;, and &quot;The Son of Man&quot;, a man in a bowler hat whose face is hidden by an apple. He worked mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and influenced pop art and conceptual art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-magritte.jpg" alt="René Magritte" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_en_buste_du_peintre_surr%C3%A9aliste_Ren%C3%A9_Magritte_(1898-1967)_le_18_octobre_1961_devant_une_de_ses_toiles,_PH19994.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>René François Ghislain Magritte was born on 21 November 1898 in Lessines, in the province of Hainaut, the oldest son of Léopold Magritte, a tailor and textile merchant, and Régina, a milliner before her marriage. Little is known of his early life. He began drawing lessons in 1910. On 24 February 1912 his mother drowned herself in the River Sambre at Châtelet, after an earlier attempt. Some writers have linked the image of her dress covering her face to paintings of 1927–28 showing people with cloth over their faces, such as Les Amants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His earliest paintings, from about 1915, were Impressionistic. He studied at the Académie Royale des Beaux-Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1916–18 but found the teaching uninspiring. In 1922 he married Georgette Berger and saw a reproduction of Giorgio de Chirico's The Song of Love, which moved him to tears. He worked as a poster designer until a contract with Galerie Le Centaure in Brussels let him paint full-time in 1926, the year of his first surreal painting, The Lost Jockey.</p><p>His first solo exhibition, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1927, was poorly reviewed, and he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he befriended André Breton and joined the Surrealist group. In 1930 he returned to Brussels and went back to advertising with his brother Paul. He stayed in Brussels during the German occupation, which led to a break with Breton. In 1946 he signed the manifesto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surrealism">Surrealism</a> in Full Sunlight. During the lean postwar years he produced fake works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Picasso</a>, Braque and de Chirico, and at the end of 1948 returned to his prewar style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Magritte showed ordinary objects in unfamiliar settings, giving new meanings to familiar things. The Treachery of Images shows a pipe above the words Ceci n'est pas une pipe; asked about it, he replied that it was not a pipe, since one could not fill it with tobacco. He often painted rocks floating in the sky. His surrealism was more representational than the automatic style of Joan Miró, and he said that his paintings were visible images that conceal nothing.</p><p>Popular interest rose in the 1960s, and he influenced pop, minimalist and conceptual art, although he rejected the connection with pop art. He died of pancreatic cancer on 15 August 1967, aged 68. The Magritte Museum opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 30 May 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “René Magritte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belgian culture has been prolific, from the painters James Ensor and <b>René Magritte</b> and the Art Nouveau of Victor Horta to the comics of Hergé, while Adolphe Sax invented the saxophone and Georges Lemaître proposed the theory of the expanding universe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brussels group included E. L. T. Mesens, <b>René Magritte</b>, Paul Nougé, Marcel Lecomte and André Souris.</blockquote><a href="/movement/surrealism">Surrealism · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lessines, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.71,3.83&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Lessines on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4637 404 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of René Magritte"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5087 674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5087 674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.71,3.83&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
